-- chem/inertia.cub — the inertia engine: how many eigenvalues of a symmetric matrix lie below a rational point,
-- exactly, with no eigenvalue computed. For a symmetric integer matrix M and λ = p/q (q > 0), N = q·M − p·1 has
-- the eigenvalues of M shifted and scaled by q > 0, so the number of eigenvalues of M below λ is the number of
-- negative eigenvalues of N. Bareiss's fraction-free elimination without pivoting (1968) yields N's leading
-- principal minors D_1 … D_n, every division exact by Sylvester's identity, every number an integer in binary
-- (chem/binary.cub). When every D_k is nonzero, Jacobi's rule (Sylvester's law of inertia applied to N = LDLᵀ,
-- whose pivots are D_k/D_{k−1}) gives the number of negative eigenvalues as the number of sign changes along
-- 1, D_1, …, D_n. When some D_k is zero the count is not read and another point is taken: the points where it
-- happens are finitely many, the roots of the leading minors. This counts the levels of any finite Hamiltonian
-- below any rational energy (docs/chemical-identity.md, "Notes, uncertainty first", the inertia engine), and
-- bisection on λ brackets each level between rationals.
import "h2sound.hypercubical"

